// Tailscale access control policy for the app-hub always-on host (P-11 / G1).
//
// WHERE THIS GOES: login.tailscale.com -> Access controls. That screen edits ONE
// document for the whole tailnet: whatever you save IS the entire policy. So this
// is the complete file, not a fragment.
//
// REPLACING THE DEFAULT POLICY WITH THIS FILE IS THE INTENDED MOVE. The default
// you see in the console is an allow-everything rule, and it is reproduced
// verbatim below -- you are not giving anything up. What you gain is tagOwners
// and nodeAttrs, without which Funnel cannot work at all.
//
// Before you save, compare against what is actually in YOUR console. If it holds
// anything not reproduced here -- subnet routes, groups, extra acls rules, an
// autoApprovers block -- carry it over. Saving discards whatever you leave out,
// and it does so without warning.
//
// The format is HuJSON: JSON that allows comments and trailing commas. The admin
// console accepts it as written, comments included.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THIS HAS TO ACHIEVE, and it is two different things on two ports:
//
//   :443  -> gateway:8001   PUBLIC via Funnel  -- anyone on the internet
//   :8443 -> n8n:5678       TAILNET ONLY       -- only your own devices
//
// The split is enforced in compose/tailscale-serve.json by which ports appear
// under "AllowFunnel". THIS file decides something different and prior: whether
// the node is ALLOWED to use Funnel at all. Funnel is off by default for a
// tailnet, and without the nodeAttrs block below it silently will not serve --
// a failure that looks like networking and is really authorization.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
{
	// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// acls -- who may reach what INSIDE the tailnet.
	//
	// This is the permissive default a personal tailnet starts with, kept
	// deliberately. Tightening it is a real option later, but doing it in the
	// same change as enabling Funnel would make a broken n8n route ambiguous:
	// you would not know whether the ACL or the serve config was at fault.
	//
	// This is what lets your laptop and phone reach n8n on :8443.
	// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
	"acls": [
		{"action": "accept", "src": ["*"], "dst": ["*:*"]},
	],

	// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// ssh -- THIS IS NOT DECORATION. It restores a default you are about to
	// destroy by saving this file at all.
	//
	// Tailscale's docs are explicit: "Tailnets that have NOT MODIFIED their
	// tailnet policy file have a default SSH policy allowing users to access
	// devices they own using check mode." The default is conditional on the
	// file being untouched -- so the first time you save ANY edit, it is gone.
	//
	// Omitting this block would therefore not leave SSH "as it was". It would
	// silently switch Tailscale SSH off across the whole tailnet, at the moment
	// you were doing something unrelated to SSH. This is the exact rule
	// Tailscale applies by default, reproduced so that saving changes nothing.
	//
	// If you have never used Tailscale SSH you can delete this block -- but
	// delete it deliberately, rather than by leaving it out and not noticing.
	// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
	"ssh": [
		{
			"action": "check",
			"src":    ["autogroup:member"],
			"dst":    ["autogroup:self"],
			"users":  ["autogroup:nonroot", "root"],
		},
	],

	// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// tagOwners -- declares tag:app-hub before anything can use it.
	//
	// A tag must be declared here or an auth key carrying it is REJECTED at
	// login, with an error that reads like a bad key rather than a missing tag.
	// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
	"tagOwners": {
		"tag:app-hub": ["autogroup:admin"],
	},

	// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// nodeAttrs -- THE LINE THAT ENABLES FUNNEL. Without it, everything else is
	// configured correctly and the public URL still does not answer.
	//
	// TARGETED AT tag:app-hub RATHER THAN autogroup:member, and the difference
	// is worth understanding because the easier option is the looser one:
	//
	//   autogroup:member  -> EVERY device you own may publish to the public
	//                        internet, including your laptop. One mistyped
	//                        `tailscale funnel` command then exposes something
	//                        you did not mean to expose.
	//   tag:app-hub       -> only this host can. Nothing else in the tailnet
	//                        gains the capability.
	//
	// The second reason to tag is not about security at all: TAGGED DEVICES DO
	// NOT HAVE KEY EXPIRY. learn/34 records "node keys expire at 180 days" as a
	// thing that will bite later -- an untagged node silently drops off the
	// tailnet roughly six months in, and the public URL stops working with no
	// change on your side. Tagging removes that failure mode entirely, which
	// matters for a host whose whole job is to stay up. Confirm it on the
	// Machines page: a tagged node shows no expiry date.
	//
	// THE COST, stated plainly: the auth key must be generated WITH this tag
	// (Settings -> Keys -> Generate -> tick Reusable, then select tag:app-hub).
	// A key without the tag produces an untagged node, Funnel stays refused, and
	// the symptom is a public URL that does not answer.
	// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
	"nodeAttrs": [
		{
			"target": ["tag:app-hub"],
			"attr":   ["funnel"],
		},
	],
}
